      "text": "|_____|_____|_____|_____|_____|_____|_____|_____|\n\nGiven that we have 9 seats labeled as |1|2|3|4|5|6|7|8|, and we want to find the probability that when we pick 4 random seats, the reserved $|y$ seats are together (i.e., in seats $|2|3|4|5$).\n\nWe can calculate this probability through two methods.\n1. **Triangulation Method**:\nThe total number of ways to choose these 4 seats out of 9 is given by $\\binom{9}{4}$.\n\nFor the intended arrangement for the 4 math majors, there is only $1$ possibility (the ones which satisfies the given conditions).\n\nFor the remaining 3 seats, we represent them as blank seats labeled as blank seats $1|2|3|4|5$. There are $5 \\times 4 \\times 3$ ways to fill these blank seats with baseball caps privately. These numbers come from having $5$ possible choices for the first hat, $4$ for the second, and $3$ for the third.\n\nTherefore, the total number of possibilities is $\\binom{9}{4}\\times 1\\times 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 110\\text{* }60=6600$.\n\n2. **Combinations**:\nAlternatively, directly calculate the number of favorable outcomes as $\\binom{7}{4}\\times\\binom{4}{4}$:\n\nStarting from any seat to pick the first math major, then for the second to pick the math major sitting next to the first, and so on. For the last math major, since there's only one empty seat left, there's only one other math major possible. All other orders for these 4 math majors count.\n\nThis gives $531\\times2$ ways.\n\n3. **Full Method of Combinatorics**:\nFirst, calculate the desired permutation sets, which is $\\binom{8}{5}\\times5!$. And then divide this across the total outcomes of $\\binom{8}{4}\\times4!$.\n\nSo the probability is:\n\\[ = \\frac{\\binom{8}{5}\\times{5!}}{\\binom{8}{4}\\times{4!}} = \\frac{56}{70} = \\boxed{\\frac{4}{5}} \\]",
