      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to seat the 9 people around a round table.**\n   Since the table is round, the total number of ways to arrange 9 people is \\((9-1)! = 8!\\).\n\n   \\[\n   8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively.**\n   Treat the four math majors as a single \"block\". This block plus the other 5 individuals gives us 6 \"items\" to arrange around the table. The number of ways to arrange these 6 \"items\" around a round table is \\((6-1)! = 5!\\).\n\n   \\[\n   5! = 120\n   \\]\n\n   Within the block, the 4 math majors can permute among themselves in \\(4!\\) ways.\n\n   \\[\n   4! = 24\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the number of favorable arrangements is:\n\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability.**\n   The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
