      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the total number of ways to arrange nine people in a round table and the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively. We will then divide the number of favorable arrangements by the total number of arrangements to find the probability.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of ways to arrange nine people in a round table. The formula for the number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people in a circle is \\((n-1)!\\). For nine people, this is:\n\\[\n(9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively. We can treat the four math majors as a single \"block\" or \"super person,\" which means we are now arranging 6 \"people\" (the block of 4 math majors and the other 5 individuals). The number of ways to arrange these 6 \"people\" in a circle is \\((6-1)! = 5!\\). Within the block, the 4 math majors can be arranged among themselves in \\(4!\\) ways. Therefore, the total number of favorable arrangements is:\n\\[\n5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n\\]\n\nFinally, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements:\n\\[\n\\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
